// A device written in Rust (examples/rust): its functions called from a
// `for` over a million entities on one thread and on many (Rust's
// allocator on the program's threads), a buffer handed to it, and Rust's
// println; the same either way, what C gives. And .png files of every
// kind read by the png crate as stb_image reads them. Needs cargo (which
// fetches the crates on the first build) and libclang (bindgen).
// REQUIRES: rust
// Its fns are read as they are built, and keep their word.
// RUN: %ent build --no-cache -I %S/../../examples/rust %S/../../examples/rust/calls.ent -o %t.exe 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=PURE --allow-empty
// RUN: %ent run -I %S/../../examples/rust %S/../../examples/rust/calls.ent > %t.one
// RUN: FileCheck %s --input-file=%t.one
// RUN: %ent run --parallel -I %S/../../examples/rust %S/../../examples/rust/calls.ent > %t.all
// RUN: cmp %t.one %t.all
// RUN: rm -rf %t.pngs && mkdir -p %t.pngs
// RUN: python3 %S/../../examples/rust/make_pngs.py %t.pngs
// RUN: cd %t.pngs && %ent run -I %S/../../examples/rust %S/../../examples/rust/pngs.ent | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=PNG
// PURE-NOT: warning
// CHECK: Rust says hello, 42
// CHECK-NEXT: mixed 8790920353524676646 squares 9604000000 divided 174998885716 dot 37492162.5
// PNG: 31 of 31 pictures the same, 26381 pixels
// PNG-NEXT: none: -1, not a png: -1, 0 pixels
